    "query_id": "05b-pvc-unbound",
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "My application cannot start because its volume claim is not working in namespace scenario-test. Diagnose the PVC and PV situation and tell me the root cause."
      }
    ],
    "final_text": "INVESTIGATION_PLAN:\n- Inspect the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) `broken-claim` in the `scenario-test` namespace to understand its configuration and status.\n- Check for any associated PersistentVolume (PV) and its binding status.\n- Investigate the storage class `fake-storage-class` mentioned in the warning event to confirm its existence and configuration.\n\nProceeding with parallel tool calls to gather the required information.### Root Cause Analysis\n\n1. **PVC Details**:\n   - The PersistentVolumeClaim `broken-claim` in the `scenario-test` namespace is in a `Pending` state.\n   - It references a storage class named `fake-storage-class`.\n   - The PVC is not bound to any PersistentVolume (PV).\n   - Warning events indicate that the storage class `fake-storage-class` does not exist.\n\n2. **Storage Class**:\n   - The storage class `fake-storage-class` is missing from the cluster. This is confirmed by the error: `Error from server (NotFound): storageclasses.storage.k8s.io \"fake-storage-class\" not found`.\n\n### Root Cause\nThe PVC `broken-claim` cannot be provisioned because the specified storage class `fake-storage-class` does not exist in the cluster. Without a valid storage class, the persistent volume controller cannot create or bind a PV for the claim.\n\n### Suggested Fix\n1. Create a valid storage class that matches the requirements of the PVC.\n2. Update the PVC to reference an existing storage class if the current one is incorrect.\n\nWould you like me to proceed with creating a storage class or updating the PVC?",
